#!/bin/bash
# 726 (Fri 27 Nov 2026 08:46, Peter by voice: correct score gets its own look - "only a certain amount of scores are actually
# realistic"; and the nil-nil idea: back the scoreline at 19, lay at 18 as time passes with no goal, "play within that hundred
# pounds"; and he cannot always watch). Added to the two football sessions that have not started. Paper/trial only. No orders.
B=/root/pe-builder
cat >> $B/BRIEF_small_football.md <<'EOF'

ADDED 27 Nov 08:50 ON PETER'S ORDER - CORRECT SCORE GETS ITS OWN LOOK (he trained on correct score trading; the method is in the project memory file correct-score-method). (1) THE MAP OF SCORES: by league and by the main-market prices before the start, how often each scoreline happened against its price, 8 years; Peter's view is that only eight to ten scorelines are realistic and the long ones are overbet by punters - if so the realistic scores are too generous: measure it, and what BACKING the realistic set paid at real prices (no lay at 5.0 or longer). (2) THE RULER: from the match result and total goals prices at each second, what each scoreline should be; how often correct score offers are left behind, before the start and after a goal. (3) PETER'S NIL-NIL TRADE, exactly as he said it: back 0-0 at the start (about 19 in his example), and as time passes with no goal the price falls, so lay part or all a tick or more lower and repeat - the risk is the stake and no more, a goal loses what is still open. Test every version on our recorded correct score ticks at the price and money actually on offer: when to get in (kick-off, 5, 10, 15 minutes), how far to let it fall before laying back (1 tick, 5%, 10%, 20%), all out or in slices, and when to stop regardless (20, 25, 30 minutes, half time). The price fall is the market paying for the risk of a goal, so on an average game it nets to nothing less costs: the question is WHICH games - use the goal signal version 3 quiet call (11.8% goals against 19.5%), our form guide, the league, the main-market prices and money in the market to pick games quieter than the price thinks. Also the same trade on 1-0 or 0-1 after a first goal, and on the current score late in a game. Count each as one trade per match; both halves of the dates; worst day. Stakes £2 to £50 (Peter's limit is £50 a bet): say how much could really be got on and off at each stake.
EOF
cat >> $B/BRIEF_form_football.md <<'EOF'

ADDED 27 Nov 08:50 - PETER CANNOT ALWAYS WATCH. The watch card must work without him: for every question on it, the stand-in from our own live figures (shots, shots on target, expected goals, the version 3 signal) answers automatically at the set moment and is logged in eye_calls as source machine; when Peter does watch, his answer is logged beside it as source Peter. So the test runs on every big game from day one and his eye is measured as an extra, not as a requirement.
EOF
echo "726 done $(date -u +%H:%M) UTC: correct score own look and nil-nil trade in the football small-markets brief ($(grep -c 'CORRECT SCORE GETS ITS OWN LOOK' $B/BRIEF_small_football.md)); watch card works without Peter ($(grep -c 'CANNOT ALWAYS WATCH' $B/BRIEF_form_football.md)); either already started: $(pgrep -fc '[r]un_small_football.sh|[r]un_form_football.sh') (0 = both read the new briefs)." >> /root/pe-queue/reports/running_now.md
cd /root/pe-queue && git add reports/running_now.md && git commit -qm "running now: 726" -q && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q
